00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.320
pipeline.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.590
But the recruiting space is
riddled with complex and messy

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:35.110
data.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.840
Take, for example, nurses.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.600
There are hundreds of different
nurse titles and hundreds

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.000
of different certifications.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.280
And that's because nurses
also have specialties

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.210
within departments, such as
the ICU that contains surgical,

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:54.680
pediatric, or neurotrauma
nurses, just to name a few.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:57.550
So when job descriptions
are vague, such as "nurse,"

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.430
and doesn't include the
department, or cases

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.640
when it says "or
equivalent experience,"

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:07.570
the process is time-consuming
and frustrating.

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:10.240
Similar to how you can find
a lot of software engineering

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:15.910
jobs, but the details matter
between mobile iOS and Android

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.700
or programming languages
such as Java or Python.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:24.350
For years, employers
have used systems

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:30.710
to help fill job openings, but
the methods were often crude.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:34.520
A computer would identify
keywords on job descriptions,

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:37.190
then determine whether or
not those exact keywords are

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:39.680
present on a resume.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:42.110
Here are some of the challenges
in developing a model

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:45.390
in recruiting for a nurse.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.930
On the resume is a job
title for an OB GYN.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:53.680
And on the job posting,
it's an LDRP nurse.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.440
There wasn't a match because
the keywords were not exactly

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:00.520
the same, even though
they're synonymous.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:02.440
This is because
at some hospitals,

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:06.370
an LDRP nurse stands for
labor, delivery, recovery,

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:11.130
and postpartum nurse, which
is similar to an OB GYN nurse.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.930
Next, it's difficult
to track all

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:16.470
the possible accredited
colleges, the various spellings

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:20.720
and abbreviations, and what
counts as "or equivalent."

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:25.020
And the same can be
applied for licenses.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:28.860
Lastly, the job posting requires
an advanced cardiovascular life

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.960
support certification.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.190
There was no match because
it's not listed on the resume.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:37.260
But most nurses
and recruiters know

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.810
that employers provide this
training for free if you don't

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:43.040
have it or need a refresher.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.090
And these are just
a few examples where

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.732
there's room for improvement.

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:52.720
As you're all aware, it's
important to think about bias

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:55.510
when using machine
learning in hiring.

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:58.990
The common issues
stem from four areas.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:01.480
Unclear use cases,
where there's too much

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:04.790
focus on the solution
instead of the problem.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:07.210
So before digging into
the various models,

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:10.600
take a human-centered approach,
and establish a clear use case

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.840
first.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.690
Misaligned expectations,
where there's a tendency

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:17.910
to just throw everything
at a model and just

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.100
let it figure it out.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:23.940
Small data sets, where there
isn't enough training data

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.410
to learn all the
various patterns.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:30.460
So make sure you have
a lot of relevant data.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:31.930
And biased data.

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:34.255
So spend time examining
the training data for bias.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:39.220
For the leaders in
our audience, these

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:40.780
are important
considerations when

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:43.870
deciding on developing
in-house or evaluating

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:46.455
technology providers.

00:07:46.455 --> 00:07:47.830
Now let's go
through how to apply

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:49.431
these methods in recruiting.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:56.340
Here's an example of a
nurse job search query.

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:57.900
Based on the data,
there's a need

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:03.290
for identifying 12-hour shifts
and narrowing location radius

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:06.180
to five miles.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:10.190
But we only know half the story
of what they're looking for

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:10.860
and not why.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:15.500
After speaking to
users, we learned

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:19.940
that the assumptions based on
just the data were misleading.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:22.700
When nurses look for jobs,
it's in terms of shifts

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:27.270
where longer shifts
also implies fewer days.

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:29.880
This nurse was actually looking
for a part time job that's

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:34.809
only two days a week and not a
60-hour per week job that pays

00:08:34.809 --> 00:08:39.190
overtime, and a need
to commute by bike

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:43.730
and not necessarily
within five miles.

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.870
By taking a human-centered
approach and speaking to users,

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:50.630
we can understand why and
develop the right solutions

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:52.400
that addresses their needs.

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.032
And then we can
revisit and understand

00:08:54.032 --> 00:08:55.490
if we're looking
at the right data.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.620
Now let's look at something
more complex where the use

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:05.390
case is screening a resume.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:10.270
Here the job description
asks for an RN

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:12.260
with a bachelor's
degree in nursing,

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.400
and provides emotional
care to patients.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:18.880
We reason that education
information would satisfy

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.470
the education requirement.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:25.650
As for emotional care,
it requires the ability

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.820
to empathize and communicate.

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:32.420
Volunteer work is probably
a strong signal for empathy.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:34.760
As for good
communication skills,

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:37.760
recent studies show that
multilingual people have

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:39.065
better communication skills.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:47.920
And you probably didn't need
to know that they're female.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.980
So our hypothesis is,
volunteer work and languages

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:53.110
are features that can
predict the ability

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:55.690
to provide emotional care.

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:57.730
And by developing
a hypothesis, it

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.770
helps identify
reasonable features

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:02.470
to begin with and
iterate on along

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:06.790
with the ability to detect if
the necessary data is missing.

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:09.610
Not to mention, it also
aims towards a less complex

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:13.150
solution, and this makes it
easier to debug and improve

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:13.720
on the model.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:21.680
Machine learning models
require a lot of relevant data.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:23.290
How much is a lot?

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:25.280
That depends on the problem.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:29.110
But the more data typically
improves your model.

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:31.960
A good rule of thumb is
to have at least thousands

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:35.830
of examples for a basic linear
model and hundreds of thousands

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:38.240
for a neural network.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:41.210
For example, in terms
of nursing jobs,

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:43.490
we'll need at least
thousands of labeled examples

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:46.980
for both the positive
and negative cases.

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:48.680
So this means
thousands of nurses

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:50.960
that can provide emotional
care and thousands that

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:55.630
can't, and across all of the
different types of nurses.

00:10:55.630 --> 00:10:57.470
So if there are 10
different types,

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:00.400
then we're looking at
least 20,000 examples

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:02.980
for a basic linear model.

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:05.100
And if a neural
network is needed,

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:09.220
then the order of magnitude
is in the millions.

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.340
So if you have
less data, consider

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:13.470
finding a partner that can
bring the necessary data

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:14.250
scale needed.

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:20.570
Now, before we go into
examining the data,

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:22.910
if everyone in the audience
can imagine a shoe.

00:11:29.222 --> 00:11:31.055
Now raise your hand if
you imagine sneakers.

00:11:35.775 --> 00:11:36.275
High heels?

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:41.180
High tops?

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:42.510
Anyone, high tops?

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:44.810
No.

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:47.900
Given that majority raise
their hands to sneakers,

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:50.900
we've immediately
introduced a bias.

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:53.960
And to add more complexity,
what color were they?

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:59.470
Here are four types of bias.

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:03.820
Something that impacts all data
collected is reporting bias.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:07.480
Reporting bias occurs when
people annotate the frequency

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:09.460
of things that doesn't
reflect the real world

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:11.800
frequency but things
that they found

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:16.210
interesting or worth noting.

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:19.810
So for this example,
for sneakers,

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:23.710
you may not know that sneakers
have rubber soles when relying

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:26.350
on the top text frequency.

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:28.810
And this is because
rubber soles is ordinary

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:32.350
and can go without saying.

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:35.830
Sampling bias where the data
source is not representative

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:37.660
of all the different variants.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:42.350
For our shoes, we only had
those that were typical to us.

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:46.280
But we were missing Oxford's,
sandals, or rain boots.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:54.440
Latent bias when the model is
only shown a specific example.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:57.573
So if the historical data
only contains sneakers,

00:12:57.573 --> 00:12:59.240
then it would be
biased towards sneakers

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:01.500
and would not label
high heels correctly.

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:07.980
And interaction bias that
occurs by the way in which users

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:09.670
interact with it.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:12.750
So in this room, if everyone
kept selecting sneakers

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:15.570
in a game, then the model
will learn from the activity

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:18.470
and be biased towards sneakers.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:21.560
And these are just a few
out of more than 100 types

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:23.990
of different human
biases that may occur

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:28.250
in machine learning data sets.

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:31.070
So make sure to
examine your data

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:34.760
and audit it for any
human biases that may

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:36.908
skew your model's predictions.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:41.940
By using a
human-centered approach,

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.550
you can establish
a clear use case

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:47.520
by speaking to users
to focus on the problem

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:51.210
and then to develop a hypothesis
to determine what features

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:53.490
and data are necessary.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:56.010
And make sure you
have a lot of data.

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:59.040
In recruiting, it's hard to have
a lot of label data in hiring

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:02.850
data, which is especially
difficult across every single

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:05.010
occupation.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:07.440
Lastly, examine the data.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:09.750
Machine learning models
will reflect the data

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:11.100
that they're trained on.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:16.170
So analyze your data carefully
to ensure you understand it.

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:18.780
And for our leaders
in the audience,

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:21.040
a lot of relevant
data is necessary.

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:22.710
So if you don't
have enough data,

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:26.130
consider finding a provider that
can bring the necessary data

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:28.440
scale and develop AI with care.

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:39.040
Our team is using AI
to enhance and expand

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:41.260
what's possible in
recruiting, which

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:45.760
is a critical function of each
and every growing business.

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.430
Now I'll go over what we've
built in Cloud Talent Solution

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:50.410
to transform the job
search experience.

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:55.270
In job search,
sometimes the results

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:57.790
don't match the
job seekers intent.

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:01.210
In turn, that misunderstanding
causes hours of work

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:03.970
to find the right job
while others give up

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:06.610
and look somewhere else.

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:08.640
This is a huge
inefficiency that I'm

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:12.450
sure frustrates not just the
job seeker but businesses

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:16.970
as well, because businesses
spend a lot of effort

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:20.420
to get traffic on their sites
but may lose the candidates

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:23.970
before they even see the job.

00:15:23.970 --> 00:15:25.800
Here's an example
of a job search

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:29.340
for nurse assistant, which is
someone who supports and helps

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:30.780
nurses.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.270
There are 114 results.

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:34.770
But it turns out,
many of these jobs

00:15:34.770 --> 00:15:37.210
are not even for the right role.

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:40.420
The top result, the top
four results actually,

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:44.380
are for nurses and
not the assistant.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:47.470
The first job is an
administrative nurse.

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:51.130
The second is a senior
nurse that's an instructor.

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:53.290
And the fourth result
is a nurse practitioner

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:55.210
that requires a master's
degree in nursing.

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:00.830
To help solve these problems,
we developed Cloud Talent

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:02.120
Solution.

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:04.940
Cloud Talent Solution
is a set of APIs

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:07.400
and using machine learning
to better understand job

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:10.450
content and job seeker intent.

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:13.120
Candidates get matched to
their ideal jobs faster,

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.640
and employers attract and
convert higher quality

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:19.240
candidates by showing
more relevant results.

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:22.300
At the core is a neural network
to understand jobs, companies,

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:24.770
skills, and education.

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:27.460
You can think of it as a
two-sided matching engine that

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:31.930
takes in the job seekers intent
and employer job openings

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:34.240
and runs them through a set
of algorithms to determine

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:36.220
if there's a match or not.

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:39.880
It's all about understanding
the semantics around people

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:42.040
and the semantics
around jobs and using

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:44.875
the power of machine learning to
make the best matches possible.

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:51.280
Here's an example of how Cloud
Talent Solution understands

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:53.340
a job seekers query.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:55.610
Let's start unpacking this.

00:16:55.610 --> 00:16:58.940
Part time CNA WA Australia.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:01.260
Part time is an employment type.

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:04.950
CNA is an acronym for the
job title certified nursing

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:06.420
assistant.

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:09.599
This means a person is looking
for all job content related

00:17:09.599 --> 00:17:14.339
to the occupation that includes
nurse aide and patient care

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:16.630
assistant.

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:21.190
WA Australia is a region and
acronym for Western Australia.

00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:23.109
This includes much
of the Outback

00:17:23.109 --> 00:17:25.210
but also contains
Perth and all the way

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:28.030
to Broome, a port
city in the North.

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:30.760
All these signals are
important to consider

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:34.600
when capturing a job seeker's
query to understand the intent

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:36.580
and return all relevant jobs.

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:42.095
Underneath the hood of
many of the features

00:17:42.095 --> 00:17:43.970
and functionalities of
Cloud Talent Solutions

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:45.810
sits machine learning.

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.540
We developed the
models specifically

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:50.870
for the recruiting domain from
heuristic to neural networks

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:54.290
that helps classify jobs so that
businesses can leverage facets

00:17:54.290 --> 00:18:00.500
in their UI, or semantics to
match the different languages

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:04.560
that employers and job seekers
speak, such as the LDRP

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:07.390
and the OB-GYN nurse.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:09.940
Here, using TensorFlow,
we're able to visualize

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:13.380
text embeddings of job titles.

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:15.840
Text embeddings are
co-occurrences of terms

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:17.820
represented in a vector.

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:20.940
Words and phrases that
appear similar in context

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:23.010
within employment-related
data are

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:26.430
trained to be close in
the embedding space.

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:28.590
The resulting dense
representation

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:31.200
allow us to train deep
neural networks that

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:32.235
takes jobs as input.

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:38.460
For example, in the
visual is a cluster

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:41.310
of nurses where the
different nurse terms are

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:45.710
close in proximity,
such as the LPN, which

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:50.300
stands for a licensed practical
nurse, and the school nurse.

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:54.710
And terms that are further away,
such as the medical technician,

00:18:54.710 --> 00:18:55.560
are less similar.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.550
My team is actively working
to improve inclusion

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:06.150
by taking a human-centered
approach by using design

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:09.210
and AI to reduce repetitive
tasks and free people

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:12.310
to focus on what humans do best.

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:16.610
They build relationships
and solve problems.

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:19.640
For job search, this means
we engage a diverse set

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:22.100
of job seekers across
different dimensions,

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:25.880
such as occupations,
gender, or career stages

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:30.650
from entry level to
those nearing retirement.

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:33.950
This helps us understand the
different use case scenarios

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:38.090
and build a rich variety
of user perspectives.

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:41.540
We're also working on improving
inclusion in our model training

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:44.690
procedures, such as
detecting and measuring bias

00:19:44.690 --> 00:19:47.570
and diversifying our data.

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:51.080
Ultimately, we think AI will
have the greatest impact when

00:19:51.080 --> 00:19:54.170
everyone can access it and
when it's built with everyone's

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:55.100
benefit in mind.

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:04.970
Over the past few
years, our team

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:06.860
has spent time with
hundreds of people

00:20:06.860 --> 00:20:09.970
to understand their
job search journey

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:11.680
I met a woman who was blind.

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:15.250
And as we were searching for
jobs across various sites,

00:20:15.250 --> 00:20:19.100
we came across a company
that she was interested in.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:22.540
But we realized that the job
detail page was not accessible,

00:20:22.540 --> 00:20:26.050
even though the rest
of the site was.

00:20:26.050 --> 00:20:29.230
While I could read the
job description to her,

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:33.360
she told me not to
bother because the career

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:37.140
site is her first impression
of a career at that company.

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:39.970
And if that is
inaccessible, then

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:42.600
what would it be like on a
daily basis working there?

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:49.600
Job search is one of the first
impressions a candidate has

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:51.310
with a business.

00:20:51.310 --> 00:20:54.130
So it's important to make
jobs on your career site

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:57.490
more discoverable to a
larger group of candidates

00:20:57.490 --> 00:20:59.380
and make the experience
more inclusive.

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:04.590
Now I'll go over a
few features we've

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:06.120
developed to help
businesses reach

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:09.692
more demographically and
cognitively diverse candidates.

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:17.610
Each year, over a quarter
of a million service members

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:22.500
transition from the US
military to the civilian world.

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:24.840
We've spoken to many
service members.

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:26.880
And it turns out
the language used

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:30.630
to describe the military
service and civilian employment

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:33.470
is not always similar.

00:21:33.470 --> 00:21:35.630
Many transitioning
service members

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:38.810
need a way to translate their
experience so that they don't

00:21:38.810 --> 00:21:42.410
miss out on great opportunities,
and businesses don't miss out

00:21:42.410 --> 00:21:44.420
on great candidates.

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:47.180
We also learned that
in the US military,

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:51.790
occupational codes are used
to identify specific jobs.

00:21:51.790 --> 00:21:54.010
To assist service
members transitioning

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:57.150
to the civilian world,
we've added military codes

00:21:57.150 --> 00:21:59.020
to our understanding
of occupations

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:02.800
to surface the
relevant civilian jobs.

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:05.530
We're thrilled to have
RecruitMilitary in the audience

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:06.310
today.

00:22:06.310 --> 00:22:06.880
Hey, Mike.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:13.530
They are a veteran
recruiting company in the US

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:16.650
that represents over
a million veterans.

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:19.930
Since implementing Cloud Talent
Solution on their job board,

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:22.620
they've seen record
highs of almost twice

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:25.790
as many applications.

00:22:25.790 --> 00:22:28.340
And we're proud to help
them with their mission

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:31.900
to connect more
transitioning veterans

00:22:31.900 --> 00:22:33.140
to their first civilian job.

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:41.330
Another example is our global
language support in our service

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:43.280
that leverages the
tight integration

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:46.520
with Google Translate
Neural Machine Translations.

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:49.610
Back in the old days,
traditional phrase-based

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:51.440
translation systems
performed their tasks

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:54.410
by breaking up sentences
into multiple chunks

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:57.920
and then translate
them, phrase by phrase.

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:01.780
This led to a disfluency
in the translation outputs

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:05.720
and was not quite like
how we humans translate.

00:23:05.720 --> 00:23:08.420
We read the whole sentence,
understand its meaning,

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:10.130
and then translate it.

00:23:10.130 --> 00:23:13.490
Neural Machine
Translation mimics that.

00:23:13.490 --> 00:23:15.400
Here's an example.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:20.830
[SPEAKING SPANISH] Neural
Machine Translations

00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:22.750
considers the entire
input sentence

00:23:22.750 --> 00:23:24.980
as a unit of translation.

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:28.870
So instead of assistant
nursing certified,

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:32.020
we're able to translate and
return certified nursing

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:34.780
assistant jobs.

00:23:34.780 --> 00:23:38.800
We also learned that across the
US in major metropolitan areas,

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:42.310
over 100 different languages
are spoken at home.

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:45.550
And in Europe, a large
portion of Europeans

00:23:45.550 --> 00:23:49.510
frequently search online
in their first language.

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:52.990
A few weeks ago, we announced
an exciting new feature.

00:23:52.990 --> 00:23:55.940
For those who prefer to search
in their first language,

00:23:55.940 --> 00:23:58.330
we also return the
relevant jobs that

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:00.938
were posted in English so
that these job seekers don't

00:24:00.938 --> 00:24:03.230
miss out on jobs that they
would have otherwise missed.

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:11.130
And finally, commuting is
one of the top considerations

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:14.610
for a job, where 85%
of US professionals

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:17.480
would take a pay cut
for a shorter commute.

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:19.910
Our commute search
feature surfaces jobs

00:24:19.910 --> 00:24:23.060
based on commute time
and transportation modes.

00:24:23.060 --> 00:24:28.000
As you can imagine, to determine
the shortest commute time,

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:31.490
you'll have to identify all
the relevant different route

00:24:31.490 --> 00:24:35.390
options between two
points to answer queries.

00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:38.440
These permutations become costly
as the scale of the problem

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:42.880
increases from one metropolitan
area to an entire country

00:24:42.880 --> 00:24:46.370
or continent where
10,000 stops already

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:49.610
yields 100 million pairs.

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:52.400
We leveraged a tight
integration with Google Maps

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:54.650
to gather this
information layering

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:59.150
in our knowledge of jobs where
we've mapped millions of jobs

00:24:59.150 --> 00:25:01.700
and developed the technology
to answer all of these commute

00:25:01.700 --> 00:25:04.880
time queries on the fly.

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:07.400
As part of our
research in speaking

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:09.860
to a broad base of
users, we learned

00:25:09.860 --> 00:25:13.920
that some people need
alternative commute methods,

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:17.120
especially those living
in low income communities

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:21.290
or the nurse that was searching
for a job within biking

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:22.820
distance.

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:26.720
Recently, we announced
new commute methods

00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:29.322
so that everyone can
find work no matter

00:25:29.322 --> 00:25:30.530
how they choose to get there.

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:36.940
Overall, our clients
are seeing great results

00:25:36.940 --> 00:25:39.700
that include reduced
development time that saves them

00:25:39.700 --> 00:25:42.940
