00:28:47.550 --> 00:28:51.430
Most companies ask individuals
to apply online today.

00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:54.600
And many of them don't
even allow that application

00:28:54.600 --> 00:28:56.160
to be mobile-friendly.

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:59.250
So there is another point of
contention for candidates.

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:02.130
If you are actually
applying on a desktop,

00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:05.160
the application can
still feel very clunky.

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:07.310
It feels outdated.

00:29:07.310 --> 00:29:10.650
It's long and laborious, and so
there is continued frustration

00:29:10.650 --> 00:29:12.090
through that process.

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:16.000
Once you actually
apply, you tend to wait,

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:17.790
and you wait some more.

00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:20.670
And ultimately,
if your background

00:29:20.670 --> 00:29:22.800
meets what the company
is looking for,

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:24.360
you hear back from a recruiter.

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:25.950
And that starts
you down the path

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:29.430
of potentially several
rounds of interviews,

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:32.130
which again can feel a
little laborious depending

00:29:32.130 --> 00:29:34.110
on the company.

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:36.210
I will say, across
our industry, there

00:29:36.210 --> 00:29:40.410
is a renewed sense of focus
on creating a better candidate

00:29:40.410 --> 00:29:41.080
experience.

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:43.230
And so that gives
me some optimism

00:29:43.230 --> 00:29:45.730
for the future of how we
can improve that experience.

00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:47.460
And I think
technology and AI can

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:49.860
be avenues for us to do that.

00:29:49.860 --> 00:29:51.000
JENNIFER SU: Thanks, Adam.

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:52.680
It's great to be
partnering with you

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:55.560
and to help improve the
candidate experience.

00:29:55.560 --> 00:29:58.260
Thom, could you share
a bit about SmashFly

00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:01.080
and how AI can help transform
recruiting for your enterprise

00:30:01.080 --> 00:30:02.216
clients?

00:30:02.216 --> 00:30:02.966
THOM KENNEY: Sure.

00:30:02.966 --> 00:30:04.383
Thanks for having
me this morning.

00:30:04.383 --> 00:30:06.810
I apologize for the glare a
little bit for those of you

00:30:06.810 --> 00:30:08.580
who are seeing
the bright lights.

00:30:08.580 --> 00:30:10.230
It is actual baldness.

00:30:10.230 --> 00:30:12.360
It's not fake.

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:16.140
SmashFly is a company that was
founded about 12 years ago.

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:17.760
And it was founded
with a purpose,

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:20.460
to really connect the right
talent, with the right hiring

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:21.990
managers, at the right time.

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:24.030
It's a very simple prospect.

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:27.090
But when you think about the
complexity of how people look

00:30:27.090 --> 00:30:30.540
for jobs, how jobs are
posted, where jobs are posted,

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:33.780
and with what Jen was showing
earlier, all the different ways

00:30:33.780 --> 00:30:37.200
that employers are using
language, and using colors

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:39.850
and styles to try
to attract talent,

00:30:39.850 --> 00:30:42.078
there's no one single best way.

00:30:42.078 --> 00:30:44.370
And when SmashFly really
started to take a look at this

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:46.470
and the technology
a few years ago,

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:49.500
we recognized there was
a huge opportunity for us

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:51.990
to leverage some of the
AI and ML techniques

00:30:51.990 --> 00:30:55.200
to learn how talent is
engaging with employers

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:58.200
but then also how employers
are engaging with talent.

00:30:58.200 --> 00:30:59.730
So when we think
about it, SmashFly

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:01.890
is in 69 countries
around the world

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:03.520
where we have
recruiters working.

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:06.270
And we have talent that
interface with us in over 180

00:31:06.270 --> 00:31:07.840
countries around the world.

00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:10.620
And if you think about the
micro-climates of recruiting

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:13.890
that that represents, it's
almost infinite in the number

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:16.230
of complications
with the way people

00:31:16.230 --> 00:31:19.660
would interface together,
the employers and the talent.

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:22.080
So when we think about
especially the machine learning

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:24.553
aspects, there's tons
of data available.

00:31:24.553 --> 00:31:26.970
And you ask any data scientist
or machine learning person,

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:29.428
and they're going to tell you
the two most important things

00:31:29.428 --> 00:31:30.890
that they need are data--

00:31:30.890 --> 00:31:33.590
lots of it-- but
data that's good.

00:31:33.590 --> 00:31:35.840
Jen talked about the
training data sets.

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:38.300
And we looked at the training
data sets that we would have

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:40.100
and the value that it brought.

00:31:40.100 --> 00:31:42.680
So we're leveraging the
AI and ML techniques

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:45.157
to really use that amount
of volumous information

00:31:45.157 --> 00:31:45.740
that we have--

00:31:45.740 --> 00:31:48.370
1.2 million hires that we've
done through the SmashFly

00:31:48.370 --> 00:31:49.460
platform--

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:53.420
and really move those forward
in a way that engages talent,

00:31:53.420 --> 00:31:56.660
engages them in a way that gets
them excited about an employer.

00:31:56.660 --> 00:31:57.860
It's not just about Cox.

00:31:57.860 --> 00:31:59.630
You don't just know
Cox Communication

00:31:59.630 --> 00:32:00.975
as your cable company.

00:32:00.975 --> 00:32:02.600
I was talking to Adam
earlier, and he's

00:32:02.600 --> 00:32:04.250
got this whole
automotive division,

00:32:04.250 --> 00:32:05.780
which was really crazy.

00:32:05.780 --> 00:32:08.780
And then print and web
media, radio media.

00:32:08.780 --> 00:32:11.150
It's a huge conglomerate
organization.

00:32:11.150 --> 00:32:13.660
Well, how does SmashFly
help Cox with that?

00:32:13.660 --> 00:32:16.670
It's like, we really try to
move that emotive action.

00:32:16.670 --> 00:32:19.760
How do you create that
connection with the employer?

00:32:19.760 --> 00:32:22.570
And there's no way to do
this in a human function.

00:32:22.570 --> 00:32:25.430
We could have hundreds of people
looking at these job postings,

00:32:25.430 --> 00:32:28.250
looking at the way people are
tracking, looking at reports.

00:32:28.250 --> 00:32:30.900
But they're not going to be
doing a good enough job for us,

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:32.660
because we need to
be moving faster.

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:34.700
We need to be looking
in places that we never

00:32:34.700 --> 00:32:36.020
thought to look before.

00:32:36.020 --> 00:32:38.090
So the AI and ML
construct's really

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:39.557
what's going to
move that forward.

00:32:39.557 --> 00:32:41.390
And if you think about
it, one of the things

00:32:41.390 --> 00:32:43.370
that we're really trying
to do, because it's

00:32:43.370 --> 00:32:45.750
that emotive response
that we want,

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:47.930
we're not trying to
eliminate the human.

00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:49.730
We actually have a
customer of ours.

00:32:49.730 --> 00:32:53.540
Global company, great company,
very recognizable brand name.

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:56.420
They took a year to
build a system that

00:32:56.420 --> 00:32:59.805
would use machine learning,
deep learning, neural networks.

00:32:59.805 --> 00:33:01.430
And they were going
to create a process

00:33:01.430 --> 00:33:03.805
by which they were going to
find the best candidates that

00:33:03.805 --> 00:33:04.903
existed out there.

00:33:04.903 --> 00:33:06.320
And they put this
system in place.

00:33:06.320 --> 00:33:08.750
They spent millions of
dollars building it.

00:33:08.750 --> 00:33:10.790
And six months after
they put it in place,

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:15.170
they realized 50% fewer people
were accepting their job

00:33:15.170 --> 00:33:16.070
offers.

00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:18.860
And the reason was they'd
eliminated the human element

00:33:18.860 --> 00:33:20.070
completely.

00:33:20.070 --> 00:33:22.160
So when we think
about it at SmashFly,

00:33:22.160 --> 00:33:24.440
all the great technology
that's out there,

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:26.720
it's not about replacing
the human element.

00:33:26.720 --> 00:33:30.110
It's about augmenting
what humans naturally do,

00:33:30.110 --> 00:33:31.890
what they do best.

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:33.950
So help the talent
find the human that's

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:37.355
trying to hire them and get
to those outcomes much faster.

00:33:37.355 --> 00:33:38.570
JENNIFER SU: Thanks, Thom.

00:33:38.570 --> 00:33:42.830
We're excited to bring AI with
the human-centered approach

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:47.450
to your clients' talent
acquisition process.

00:33:47.450 --> 00:33:50.840
Now I'd love to hear from
you both about the importance

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:55.370
of responsible AI and
inclusive hiring practices.

00:33:55.370 --> 00:33:57.320
Thom, would you like
to start with why

00:33:57.320 --> 00:34:01.460
this is important to SmashFly
and to your customers?

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:05.980
THOM KENNEY: Everything
about bias in human resources

00:34:05.980 --> 00:34:09.639
and in recruiting is one of the
biggest topics we have today.

00:34:09.639 --> 00:34:11.770
How do you create that
inclusive environment?

00:34:11.770 --> 00:34:13.830
How do you create diversity?

00:34:13.830 --> 00:34:15.820
And if anybody watched
the news this morning,

00:34:15.820 --> 00:34:18.880
Gizmodo posted
something that Congress

00:34:18.880 --> 00:34:21.219
has introduced a bill
that will require

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:25.980
companies that do AI to document
how it is eliminating bias.

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:27.580
I mean, that's a
pretty big step,

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:29.770
because now we're
recognizing the power of AI

00:34:29.770 --> 00:34:31.300
and the power of
machine learning.

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:33.050
And now you've got
people worried about it

00:34:33.050 --> 00:34:34.239
and starting to regulate it.

00:34:34.239 --> 00:34:36.020
Whether that's good
or bad is indifferent.

00:34:36.020 --> 00:34:38.679
But it really raises
the level of awareness

00:34:38.679 --> 00:34:42.610
about how diversity, inclusion,
and bias works in systems.

00:34:42.610 --> 00:34:44.510
There's a great
company out there,

00:34:44.510 --> 00:34:48.040
Intel, where their CEO
a few years ago said,

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:52.449
we want to create a diverse
workforce that exceeds

00:34:52.449 --> 00:34:54.810
the standards across the board.

00:34:54.810 --> 00:34:58.090
If the standard in our industry
is 30% women engineers,

00:34:58.090 --> 00:34:58.960
we want to do that.

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:00.790
If the standard
across the industry

00:35:00.790 --> 00:35:03.950
is 50% African-American,
we want to do that.

00:35:03.950 --> 00:35:06.380
So not creating some
pie in the sky numbers,

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:08.990
but creating something that
says the industry standards are

00:35:08.990 --> 00:35:11.470
this, and we're not there.

00:35:11.470 --> 00:35:13.900
And the "Forbes" articles
that you read about this

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:14.820
are just fascinating.

00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:17.830
The CEO created a Big,
Hairy, Audacious Goal.

00:35:17.830 --> 00:35:20.050
Anybody's read
Kotter from Harvard

00:35:20.050 --> 00:35:23.410
knows the BHAG is a way to
really inspire people and get

00:35:23.410 --> 00:35:24.850
them to move to a goal.

00:35:24.850 --> 00:35:28.150
And they set the goal for
2020, but they achieved it long

00:35:28.150 --> 00:35:29.520
before that.

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:31.540
And so when we think
about it, working with Cox

00:35:31.540 --> 00:35:33.880
and working with other
customers that we have,

00:35:33.880 --> 00:35:37.570
they want the diverse
and inclusive workforces.

00:35:37.570 --> 00:35:39.873
They bring different skill sets.

00:35:39.873 --> 00:35:41.290
I don't know how
many of you would

00:35:41.290 --> 00:35:44.860
think that my first career
was as an opera singer.

00:35:44.860 --> 00:35:45.770
I had a great time.

00:35:45.770 --> 00:35:48.190
I sang in seven different
languages, sang in Europe,

00:35:48.190 --> 00:35:50.130
sang in the US, had
a really great time.

00:35:50.130 --> 00:35:51.430
I had a tumor in my throat.

00:35:51.430 --> 00:35:52.413
Ended my opera career.

00:35:52.413 --> 00:35:54.580
Technology was a cool thing
at the time in the '90s.

00:35:54.580 --> 00:35:55.747
So I thought, what the heck?

00:35:55.747 --> 00:35:56.840
I'll do that.

00:35:56.840 --> 00:35:58.420
But it's one of
those things where

00:35:58.420 --> 00:35:59.830
I think about
things a little bit

00:35:59.830 --> 00:36:02.170
differently than maybe a
classically trained engineer

00:36:02.170 --> 00:36:03.040
would.

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:05.770
And that just little
bit of diversity with me

00:36:05.770 --> 00:36:08.710
adds some color to
the employee base.

00:36:08.710 --> 00:36:11.260
And when we leverage tools,
especially the Google search

00:36:11.260 --> 00:36:13.420
that we put in
place for Cox, we're

00:36:13.420 --> 00:36:15.070
leveraging a way
that we're trying

00:36:15.070 --> 00:36:18.490
to create that diverse and that
inclusive environment in ways

00:36:18.490 --> 00:36:20.380
that we don't want
the humans just

00:36:20.380 --> 00:36:21.605
to make that judgment call.

00:36:21.605 --> 00:36:23.980
Because even if you make a
judgment call about diversity,

00:36:23.980 --> 00:36:26.110
you're still using
your inherent bias

00:36:26.110 --> 00:36:27.710
to make that judgment call.

00:36:27.710 --> 00:36:31.060
So let's build systems and
processes that actually empower

00:36:31.060 --> 00:36:33.190
the human engagement
in a way that we

00:36:33.190 --> 00:36:35.950
develop these inclusive
relationships with people.

00:36:35.950 --> 00:36:38.710
A diverse workforce,
an inclusive workforce

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:41.620
is one that actually
transforms industries.

00:36:41.620 --> 00:36:43.940
We see this research everywhere.

00:36:43.940 --> 00:36:46.660
And our ability to provide
that to our customers

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:48.280
is one of the most
important things

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:49.558
that we can do at SmashFly.

00:36:49.558 --> 00:36:50.350
JENNIFER SU: Great.

00:36:50.350 --> 00:36:51.640
Thank you, Thom.

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:53.830
We're excited and share
this vision with you.

00:36:53.830 --> 00:36:57.580
Adam, if you can speak more
on this topic as an enterprise

00:36:57.580 --> 00:37:01.990
customer of the technology,
what does inclusive hiring

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:04.720
mean to Cox as a hiring
company and as a business?

00:37:04.720 --> 00:37:05.560
ADAM GLASSMAN: Sure.

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:08.230
So the important thing
to understand about Cox

00:37:08.230 --> 00:37:11.950
is that diversity and
inclusion are not buzzwords.

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:12.850
It's not PR.

00:37:12.850 --> 00:37:13.780
It's not publicity.

00:37:13.780 --> 00:37:17.020
It really is a core foundational
value within the company.

00:37:17.020 --> 00:37:21.120
And it's been held since its
very inception 120 years ago.

00:37:21.120 --> 00:37:24.370
And because it is a core
foundational value for us,

00:37:24.370 --> 00:37:28.150
we act with intention to try to
make the workplace as inclusive

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:29.410
as possible.

00:37:29.410 --> 00:37:31.837
And that takes several
different phases.

00:37:31.837 --> 00:37:33.670
If you think about the
journey that somebody

00:37:33.670 --> 00:37:36.250
would take at the very
early point of connecting

00:37:36.250 --> 00:37:39.670
with a brand and
applying for a position,

00:37:39.670 --> 00:37:43.030
it's that career site
experience as well that Jen

00:37:43.030 --> 00:37:45.010
had talked about
with accessibility

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:46.990
and making sure that
their search is connecting

00:37:46.990 --> 00:37:48.670
in the way that
is good for them.

00:37:48.670 --> 00:37:51.490
It's the language we use
on our job descriptions.

00:37:51.490 --> 00:37:55.300
It's the way a recruiter
evaluates talent and recommends

00:37:55.300 --> 00:37:57.340
them to a hiring manager.

00:37:57.340 --> 00:38:00.220
It's the way a hiring
manager evaluates talent

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:03.790
to ultimately make the offer
for someone to join the company.

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:07.030
It's the way an employee
experiences their work

00:38:07.030 --> 00:38:07.960
within the company.

00:38:07.960 --> 00:38:11.590
And so at Cox, we have steps
along these different journey

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:13.870
paths to make sure that
we are providing the most

00:38:13.870 --> 00:38:16.910
inclusive area as possible.

00:38:16.910 --> 00:38:19.840
We know that as a
business, we want

00:38:19.840 --> 00:38:22.960
to have those diverse ideas,
and those diverse perspectives,

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:23.915
and those backgrounds.

00:38:23.915 --> 00:38:25.540
Number one, it's the
right thing to do.

00:38:25.540 --> 00:38:28.720
But number two, it does
make our business stronger.

00:38:28.720 --> 00:38:32.200
From a cultural perspective,
that is something

00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:34.520
that we act with intention on.

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:37.870
And then, we know
that employees, when

00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:41.350
they feel warm and welcomed
in an organization,

00:38:41.350 --> 00:38:43.450
and they can feel comfortable
in their own skin,

00:38:43.450 --> 00:38:44.770
they perform at a high level.

00:38:44.770 --> 00:38:46.765
And they can do amazing things.

00:38:46.765 --> 00:38:50.230
At Cox, we do have
gender diversity

00:38:50.230 --> 00:38:51.310
at the executive level.

00:38:51.310 --> 00:38:53.990
Which I'm proud to say,
of our four divisions,

00:38:53.990 --> 00:38:57.890
we have Chief Human Resource
officers at each of them,

00:38:57.890 --> 00:39:00.460
and all four of them are female.

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:02.150
Across the board,
we've been recognized

00:39:02.150 --> 00:39:04.910
by a number of different
diversity organizations

00:39:04.910 --> 00:39:06.830
for our efforts,
including the Human Rights

00:39:06.830 --> 00:39:10.628
Campaign, "Forbes,"
DiversityInc, among others.

00:39:10.628 --> 00:39:11.420
JENNIFER SU: Great.

00:39:11.420 --> 00:39:12.680
Thank you, Adam.

00:39:12.680 --> 00:39:15.560
It's really inspiring to hear
about Cox's diversity inclusion

00:39:15.560 --> 00:39:16.980
values.

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:20.000
Now I'd like to hear from you
both about why you were excited

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:23.150
about our recent integration
and the inclusive features,

00:39:23.150 --> 00:39:27.270
such as the veterans job search,
the multilingual support,

00:39:27.270 --> 00:39:28.400
and commute search.

00:39:28.400 --> 00:39:29.940
Thom, would you like to start?

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:31.940
THOM KENNEY: So one of
the biggest things for us

00:39:31.940 --> 00:39:34.730
when we were looking at
the Google search was,

00:39:34.730 --> 00:39:36.950
what do we buy, and
what do we build?

00:39:36.950 --> 00:39:39.170
And when we looked at
our implementation,

00:39:39.170 --> 00:39:42.500
it was obvious to us that when
you talk about natural language

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:44.930
processing, you talk
about semantic analysis,

00:39:44.930 --> 00:39:46.870
you talk about just
search in general,

00:39:46.870 --> 00:39:49.200
Google's the world
leader in that space.

00:39:49.200 --> 00:39:51.920
So when we thought about, how
do we develop a better search

00:39:51.920 --> 00:39:56.030
capability for these jobs, it
was a clear cut answer for us

00:39:56.030 --> 00:39:57.840
to partner with Google on this.

00:39:57.840 --> 00:40:00.440
And when you look at
our base of customers,

00:40:00.440 --> 00:40:02.390
like I mentioned
earlier, 69 countries

00:40:02.390 --> 00:40:04.010
we have recruiters working in.

00:40:04.010 --> 00:40:07.160
The ability to leverage the
multi-language capabilities

00:40:07.160 --> 00:40:09.740
is huge for us, and it's
huge for our customers.

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:11.630
To be able to say
very, very simply,

00:40:11.630 --> 00:40:14.270
I need to find a job
with the language

00:40:14.270 --> 00:40:16.190
that I'm most
comfortable in, maybe

00:40:16.190 --> 00:40:18.410
with a second language
shown to me that I'm also

00:40:18.410 --> 00:40:20.900
comfortable in, in
multilingual societies,

00:40:20.900 --> 00:40:23.305
and be able to create that
connection very, very quickly.

00:40:23.305 --> 00:40:24.680
And when you think
about it, even

00:40:24.680 --> 00:40:27.920
from the most simplest
things, the user experience,

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:30.710
today with most search
systems, you can say,

00:40:30.710 --> 00:40:32.900
I want to find an
engineering job.

00:40:32.900 --> 00:40:35.420
Well, if you put in
"software engineer,"

00:40:35.420 --> 00:40:38.870
and it's a basic Boolean
and/or construct,

00:40:38.870 --> 00:40:41.510
you're going to end up with job
postings with product people,

00:40:41.510 --> 00:40:44.240
and marketing people, and
salespeople because they're all

00:40:44.240 --> 00:40:46.488
going to say, has to
work well with engineers

00:40:46.488 --> 00:40:47.780
if you're a technology company.

00:40:47.780 --> 00:40:49.655
So you're going to get
all these jobs come up

00:40:49.655 --> 00:40:51.980
because it's not smart enough
to really understand it.

00:40:51.980 --> 00:40:54.440
And if you do it
just by job title,

00:40:54.440 --> 00:40:56.270
then if you put
engineer in that search,

00:40:56.270 --> 00:40:58.020
you're going to miss
all the jobs that are

00:40:58.020 --> 00:40:59.630
listed as software developer.

00:40:59.630 --> 00:41:01.670
So it's not really
going to help you.

00:41:01.670 --> 00:41:04.250
One of the things that we saw
with Google was the ability

00:41:04.250 --> 00:41:07.010
to say, well, I'm looking
for an engineer position.

00:41:07.010 --> 00:41:11.143
And you type it in with
E-N-G-I-N-E-R. You misspell it.

00:41:11.143 --> 00:41:11.810
Well guess what?

00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:14.390
You still get the relevant
engineer positions

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:15.790
that still come up.

00:41:15.790 --> 00:41:17.720
But one of the bigger
things that we saw,

00:41:17.720 --> 00:41:20.690
it's near and dear to my heart,
the military occupational

00:41:20.690 --> 00:41:23.520
specialty codes and the
ability to search for that.

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:27.050
And I've been a part time
military guy for 23 years now.

00:41:27.050 --> 00:41:29.420
I'm still an executive officer
of an airborne battalion

00:41:29.420 --> 00:41:30.440
in New Jersey.

00:41:30.440 --> 00:41:32.330
And I know how hard
it is, not just

00:41:32.330 --> 00:41:35.060
for people that are
transitioning from active duty,

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:38.210
but people who are reservists
who have spent years trying

00:41:38.210 --> 00:41:40.640
to build up some skills
in the Army Reserve

00:41:40.640 --> 00:41:42.380
that they don't know
how to translate

00:41:42.380 --> 00:41:44.000
into the civilian world.

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:45.915
And a lot of these
guys struggle.

00:41:45.915 --> 00:41:50.170
Especially since 2003, I
was deployed for about 4

00:41:50.170 --> 00:41:53.482
and 1/2 years up to about 2014.

00:41:53.482 --> 00:41:55.940
And those are tough deployments,
and those are tough things

00:41:55.940 --> 00:41:57.140
to come home to.

00:41:57.140 --> 00:42:00.603
And you don't always know
how to make that transition.

00:42:00.603 --> 00:42:02.020
Even if you're a
part time person,

00:42:02.020 --> 00:42:04.460
and you go away for 15
months and you come back,

00:42:04.460 --> 00:42:07.040
your job's gone, or
your wife's gone,

00:42:07.040 --> 00:42:08.750
or there's a challenge
in your life.

00:42:08.750 --> 00:42:11.370
And you've got to find
that next opportunity.

00:42:11.370 --> 00:42:15.620
Well, helping people translate
that into a real valuable job

00:42:15.620 --> 00:42:19.612
prospectus is hugely important
for the veterans community.

00:42:19.612 --> 00:42:20.570
And it was interesting.

00:42:20.570 --> 00:42:22.500
Last night, we were
walking back from dinner.

00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:25.190
And I was walking down the
streets of San Francisco.

00:42:25.190 --> 00:42:28.250
And I was thinking about
this inclusion and diversity,

00:42:28.250 --> 00:42:29.995
and what am I going
to talk about?

00:42:29.995 --> 00:42:31.370
And it was
interesting, because I

00:42:31.370 --> 00:42:34.160
noticed as I walked,
probably 10%,

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:38.000
15% of the folks that I saw
on the side of the street,

00:42:38.000 --> 00:42:41.810
they're wearing a jacket from
the Gulf War in the '90s.

00:42:41.810 --> 00:42:43.940
They're wearing a jacket
from the early days

00:42:43.940 --> 00:42:47.030
of the wars in Iraq or
Afghanistan or a recent jacket.

00:42:47.030 --> 00:42:48.980
Because if you're
a military person,

00:42:48.980 --> 00:42:51.920
you actually know the different
uniforms that have been issued.

00:42:51.920 --> 00:42:55.250
And you know by the
uniform what time period

00:42:55.250 --> 00:42:57.770
that uniform was designed for.

00:42:57.770 --> 00:43:01.910
And I had to think to myself,
we've done 1.2 million jobs

00:43:01.910 --> 00:43:03.900
for people around the world.

00:43:03.900 --> 00:43:07.580
How many of those people that
are on the street are veterans?

00:43:07.580 --> 00:43:11.060
And how many of those people,
if we knew how to reach them,

00:43:11.060 --> 00:43:13.130
and we could give
them the capability

00:43:13.130 --> 00:43:16.670
to find that right next
opportunity, how many of them

00:43:16.670 --> 00:43:18.015
would still be on the street?

00:43:18.015 --> 00:43:20.390
And how many of them could we
help lead really productive

00:43:20.390 --> 00:43:22.910
lives and be a part
of our society?

00:43:22.910 --> 00:43:25.930
Because that's what diversity
and inclusion is all about.

00:43:25.930 --> 00:43:27.680
It's about finding the
right opportunities

00:43:27.680 --> 00:43:29.750
for the right people
and the right companies

00:43:29.750 --> 00:43:34.040
and giving them something to be
proud about every single day.

00:43:34.040 --> 00:43:36.560
ADAM GLASSMAN: Yeah, I agree
with everything that you said,

00:43:36.560 --> 00:43:37.110
Thom.

00:43:37.110 --> 00:43:40.360
Two additional features that I
know I've been impressed with

00:43:40.360 --> 00:43:43.860
are that commute search and the
multi-language capabilities.

00:43:43.860 --> 00:43:45.950
So if you start with
the commute search,

00:43:45.950 --> 00:43:48.470
one of my jobs in
employment brand

00:43:48.470 --> 00:43:51.080
is to tell a very real,
authentic story of who

00:43:51.080 --> 00:43:53.870
we are as a company and
match that with what

00:43:53.870 --> 00:43:55.230
the candidate is looking for.

00:43:55.230 --> 00:43:57.035
So we're not trying to oversell.

00:43:57.035 --> 00:43:58.910
We're trying to actually
tell a real story so

00:43:58.910 --> 00:44:00.980
that the candidate
is more informed,

00:44:00.980 --> 00:44:03.380
and they ultimately
accept a job at a company

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:05.120
that they're looking for.

00:44:05.120 --> 00:44:08.150
From a commute perspective,
look, let's be honest.

00:44:08.150 --> 00:44:10.400
In the majority of our
cities, traffic stinks.

00:44:10.400 --> 00:44:14.060
And so, who wants to sit for
two hours in rush hour traffic

00:44:14.060 --> 00:44:15.570
going back and forth to a job?

00:44:15.570 --> 00:44:18.033
Most people are looking for
a very specific commute time.

00:44:18.033 --> 00:44:20.450
And we can't always change
where our office locations are,

00:44:20.450 --> 00:44:22.325
and we don't always have
remote work and work

00:44:22.325 --> 00:44:23.900
from home opportunities.

00:44:23.900 --> 00:44:26.480
So that commute search is
a very important factor

00:44:26.480 --> 00:44:29.810
in what people are looking
for in a career and in a job.

00:44:29.810 --> 00:44:31.640
Because the worst
scenario for us

00:44:31.640 --> 00:44:34.820
is that somebody would
ultimately accept the job,

00:44:34.820 --> 00:44:37.250
and they were really looking
for a 15-minute commute,

00:44:37.250 --> 00:44:38.652
but it turns out it's two hours.

00:44:38.652 --> 00:44:40.610
And two months down the
road, they're miserable

00:44:40.610 --> 00:44:41.300
and they quit.

00:44:41.300 --> 00:44:43.230
And that doesn't help anybody.

00:44:43.230 --> 00:44:45.688
You have to start the process
all over again on both sides.

00:44:45.688 --> 00:44:47.397
So that's the worst
case scenario for us.

00:44:47.397 --> 00:44:48.814
And I think that
commute search is

00:44:48.814 --> 00:44:50.230
another one of
those features that

00:44:50.230 --> 00:44:52.550
help improve the experience
and bring that information

00:44:52.550 --> 00:44:55.520
right into the
candidate's fingertips.

00:44:55.520 --> 00:44:58.130
The second piece is the
multi-language capabilities.

00:44:58.130 --> 00:45:01.350
And so Jen talked a little
bit about that earlier,

00:45:01.350 --> 00:45:05.090
but if we looked at five random
companies in the country,

00:45:05.090 --> 00:45:07.520
and they had the
same five positions,

00:45:07.520 --> 00:45:09.410
I bet you dollars to
donuts that they'd all

00:45:09.410 --> 00:45:10.920
title them differently.

00:45:10.920 --> 00:45:13.060
So five companies with
the same five jobs,

00:45:13.060 --> 00:45:14.810
they'd all have
different titles on them.

00:45:14.810 --> 00:45:16.400
And so that's in
the same language.

00:45:16.400 --> 00:45:19.100
Now imagine if you don't
speak that language natively.

00:45:19.100 --> 00:45:22.318
We know that translating
isn't always one to one.

00:45:22.318 --> 00:45:23.360
The grammar is different.

00:45:23.360 --> 00:45:24.373
The syntax is different.

00:45:24.373 --> 00:45:26.790
And so if you're trying to
search in your native language,

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:28.582
which isn't necessarily
the native language

00:45:28.582 --> 00:45:30.860
that the company has
posted in, good luck

00:45:30.860 --> 00:45:32.388
trying to find that job match.

00:45:32.388 --> 00:45:34.430
And so this is another
one of those features that

00:45:34.430 --> 00:45:37.250
puts the information at
the candidate's fingertips

00:45:37.250 --> 00:45:38.620
to help improve the experience.

00:45:38.620 --> 00:45:40.931
So I'm excited
about both of those.

00:45:40.931 --> 00:45:43.830
JENNIFER SU: Adam, Thom, thank
you both for joining us today.

00:45:43.830 --> 00:45:47.460
We're excited to be
working alongside with you

00:45:47.460 --> 00:45:50.400
to connect more
candidates with jobs.

00:45:50.400 --> 00:45:52.960
Please give them around of
applause, to Adam and Thom.

00:45:52.960 --> 00:45:54.002
ADAM GLASSMAN: Thank you.

00:45:57.930 --> 00:46:00.150
JENNIFER SU: So
you've heard how AI

00:46:00.150 --> 00:46:04.020
can help with inclusive hiring
and how critical it is to get

00:46:04.020 --> 00:46:06.040
this right for our customers.

00:46:06.040 --> 00:46:07.650
Now I'll run through
a few takeaways,

00:46:07.650 --> 00:46:11.190
and then we can get
to your questions.

00:46:11.190 --> 00:46:13.650
To wrap up, the
definition of diversity

00:46:13.650 --> 00:46:17.010
is evolving to be
more inclusive.

00:46:17.010 --> 00:46:19.530
And it's not only the
right thing to do,

00:46:19.530 --> 00:46:22.010
but it's also good for business.

00:46:22.010 --> 00:46:26.250
AI can transform recruiting,
but applying AI to recruiting

00:46:26.250 --> 00:46:29.370
has its risk and needs
to be handled with care.

00:46:29.370 --> 00:46:33.640
By using a human-centered
approach and speaking to users,

00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:35.470
you can establish
a clear use case

00:46:35.470 --> 00:46:37.390
and develop a
hypothesis to determine

00:46:37.390 --> 00:46:39.940
what features and
data are necessary,

00:46:39.940 --> 00:46:42.670
then ensure you have a
lot of relevant data,

00:46:42.670 --> 00:46:45.160
and examine that data.

00:46:45.160 --> 00:46:47.410
If you're considering
recruitment technology,

00:46:47.410 --> 00:46:51.190
ensure your providers can
bring the necessary data scale

00:46:51.190 --> 00:46:54.060
and develop AI with care.

00:46:54.060 --> 00:46:57.930
Cloud Talent Solution is
transforming recruiting with AI

00:46:57.930 --> 00:46:59.910
and inclusive features, .

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:03.090
And we're seeing
positive results.

00:47:03.090 --> 00:47:06.090
Lastly, it's
important to remember,

00:47:06.090 --> 00:47:10.020
as creators or
influencers of technology,

00:47:10.020 --> 00:47:13.050
it's our responsibility
to develop with inclusion

00:47:13.050 --> 00:47:14.830
as part of the design.

00:47:14.830 --> 00:47:15.330
Thank you.

00:47:15.330 --> 00:47:18.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

